Reasoning: A cert-date error at a sharp timestamp means the served leaf likely expired and auto-renewal silently failed; confirm the served leaf's notAfter first.
<|call:check_cert_expiry|> {"host": "api.shopflow.io", "port": 443}
System Response: served leaf notAfter=2026-06-26T02:00:00Z (expired 25h ago), serial=0x3f1aA1; chain otherwise trusted.
Recovery reasoning: Expiry confirmed, but the cron may have issued a fresh cert that was never loaded. Check the cert sitting on disk before reissuing anything.
<|call:inspect_chain|> {"path": "/etc/ssl/shopflow/fullchain.pem", "mode": "local"}
System Response: on-disk leaf notAfter=2026-09-24 (valid 89d), serial=0x7c92E9 — disk cert is fresh and DIFFERS from the served serial.
Recovery reasoning: Renewal succeeded on disk; nginx kept the old cert in memory because the reload hook never fired. Reload to pick up the fresh leaf, validating config first.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "nginx", "validate_first": true}
System Response: config test OK; reloaded; api.shopflow.io now serves serial 0x7c92E9 notAfter 2026-09-24; ERR_CERT_DATE_INVALID cleared.
Resolution: Root cause was a successful renewal that never triggered a proxy reload; reloading loaded the already-issued leaf — no key or chain change.

Reasoning: The error names a SAN mismatch, but I verify overall cert health first so I do not chase a phantom expiry.
<|call:check_cert_expiry|> {"host": "checkout-v2.shopflow.io", "port": 443}
System Response: [OK] cert valid, notAfter 2026-08-30 (64d), chain trusted, healthcheck HTTP 200 — looks green.
Recovery reasoning: Expiry and chain are fine; the green health check masks the real fault, which is the SAN set on the served cert. Enumerate its SANs against the requested host.
<|call:verify_san_match|> {"host": "checkout-v2.shopflow.io", "cert_host": "checkout-v2.shopflow.io"}
System Response: served CN=checkout.shopflow.io; SAN=[checkout.shopflow.io, www.checkout.shopflow.io]; requested host NOT present in SAN.
Recovery reasoning: The new hostname was never added when the cert was last issued. Reissue including the new SAN while keeping the existing entries so current traffic is untouched.
<|call:reissue_certificate|> {"cn": "checkout.shopflow.io", "sans": ["checkout.shopflow.io", "www.checkout.shopflow.io", "checkout-v2.shopflow.io"], "reload": true}
System Response: issued, SAN now includes checkout-v2; reloaded; curl to checkout-v2.shopflow.io returns 200, no SAN error.
Resolution: Root cause was the new hostname missing from the cert SAN list; a reissue added it without dropping the existing SANs.

Reasoning: Works in a browser but fails PKIX in Java is the classic missing-intermediate signature — browsers AIA-fetch intermediates, Java will not. Inspect the served chain depth.
<|call:inspect_chain|> {"host": "vault.internal.corp", "port": 443}
System Response: chain depth=1; served leaf only (CN=vault.internal.corp); issuer 'Corp Issuing CA G2' NOT present in chain.
Recovery reasoning: The gateway was deployed with a leaf-only PEM. Locate the matching intermediate in the CA store before rebuilding the bundle.
<|call:inspect_trust_store|> {"ca": "Corp Issuing CA G2"}
System Response: intermediate present at /etc/pki/intermediates/corp-issuing-g2.pem (notAfter 2031); not included in the gateway bundle.
Recovery reasoning: Append the intermediate to the served chain and reload so strict validators get the full path; the root stays unchanged.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "gateway", "bundle": "leaf+corp-issuing-g2", "validate_first": true}
System Response: chain now depth=2 leaf->Corp Issuing CA G2->Corp Root; openssl verify OK; billing-worker PKIX error cleared.
Resolution: Root cause was a leaf-only deployment after rotation; appending the intermediate restored a complete chain for strict clients like Java, trust root untouched.

Reasoning: 'not yet valid' from a single node against a freshly issued cert points at that node's clock being behind the cert's notBefore, not a cert fault. Probe the handshake detail from etl-07.
<|call:tls_handshake_probe|> {"host": "s3-proxy.corp", "from": "etl-07"}
System Response: handshake_failure: certificate notBefore=2026-06-25T00:00Z > local time 2026-06-22T19:41Z; clock appears ~5 days behind.
Recovery reasoning: The valid cert reads as future-dated because the node clock is slow. Confirm NTP state before forcing any step.
<|call:check_host_clock|> {"host": "etl-07"}
System Response: ntpd inactive; offset -432000s vs pool; last successful sync 9 days ago.
Recovery reasoning: NTP died and the clock drifted ~5 days. Re-sync time; leave the certificate alone since it is valid everywhere else.
<|call:sync_ntp|> {"host": "etl-07", "step": true}
System Response: ntpd restarted; clock stepped +5d to 2026-06-27T14:02Z; offset <50ms; etl-07 now completes the handshake to s3-proxy.corp.
Resolution: Root cause was NTP failure on etl-07 leaving the clock 5 days slow so a valid cert looked not-yet-valid; re-syncing time fixed it without altering the certificate.

Reasoning: Browsers pass but one legacy client fails right after a hardening change — likely we removed a TLS version or cipher the old client needs. Check what the LB now offers.
<|call:check_tls_versions|> {"host": "lb.shopflow.io", "port": 443}
System Response: [OK] TLS1.2 enabled, TLS1.3 enabled; cert valid; modern clients negotiate AES-GCM fine — looks healthy.
Recovery reasoning: Modern handshake health is green, hiding the issue: the legacy partner likely needs a CBC suite we just dropped. Replay their client hello to see the offered ciphers.
<|call:tls_handshake_probe|> {"host": "lb.shopflow.io", "client_profile": "edi.partnerco.net", "simulate": true}
System Response: client hello max TLS1.2, ciphers=[AES128-SHA] (CBC only); server now offers AES-GCM suites only -> no shared cipher -> handshake_failure.
Recovery reasoning: They speak TLS1.2 but only the CBC AES128-SHA cipher hardening removed. Re-add it scoped to their source IP so the PCI-compliant main listener stays GCM-only.
<|call:update_cipher_policy|> {"listener": "partner-edi", "allow_source": "203.0.113.40/32", "min_version": "TLS1.2", "extra_ciphers": ["AES128-SHA"]}
System Response: scoped listener created; partner source negotiates TLS1.2 + AES128-SHA; main listener unchanged (GCM-only, TLS1.2 floor).
Resolution: Root cause was the partner's only supported cipher (CBC AES128-SHA) being stripped during hardening; a source-scoped re-add restored them without weakening the PCI main listener.

Reasoning: One caller failing client-cert verification right after its redeploy suggests it now presents a bad or wrong-CA client cert. Inspect what ledger-svc presents.
<|call:verify_client_cert|> {"caller": "ledger-svc", "target": "payments-sidecar"}
System Response: client cert CN=ledger-svc presented; chain verify FAILED: issuer 'Dev Mesh CA' not in server trust bundle (expects 'Prod Mesh CA').
Recovery reasoning: The redeploy pulled a client cert minted by the Dev CA — a secret mixup — so prod rejects it. Confirm which secret the new pod mounted before reissuing.
<|call:get_certificate_resource|> {"workload": "ledger-svc", "field": "issuerRef"}
System Response: pod mounts secret 'ledger-tls-dev' (issuerRef=ClusterIssuer/dev-mesh-ca); expected 'ledger-tls' (issuerRef=ClusterIssuer/prod-mesh-ca).
Recovery reasoning: The Deployment references the dev secret name. Repoint to the prod-issued secret and roll, keeping mTLS STRICT so no plaintext window opens.
<|call:patch_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "Deployment", "name": "ledger-svc", "patch": {"volumes.tls.secretName": "ledger-tls"}, "rollout": true}
System Response: rolled; ledger-svc now presents the Prod Mesh CA cert; payments-sidecar verifies the client cert; calls return 200.
Resolution: Root cause was the redeploy mounting the dev-CA client cert secret; repointing to the prod-issued secret restored mTLS verification with STRICT mode intact.

Reasoning: Must-Staple plus intermittent Firefox OCSP errors means the server sometimes returns no valid stapled response. Check the stapled response we serve.
<|call:query_ocsp|> {"host": "www.shopflow.io", "mode": "stapled"}
System Response: [GOOD] OCSP response stapled, status=good, thisUpdate 50m ago, signature valid — this sample looks healthy.
Recovery reasoning: A single good sample hides the intermittency; only some LB nodes likely have a fresh staple. With Must-Staple a missing staple is a hard fail. Check staple freshness across the pool.
<|call:query_ocsp|> {"host": "www.shopflow.io", "mode": "stapled", "across_pool": true}
System Response: 4 backends: 3 serve a cached good staple; lb-2 returns NO stapled response (nextUpdate passed 12m ago, responder fetch failing).
Recovery reasoning: lb-2's OCSP cache expired and its responder refresh is failing, so ~25-30% of handshakes go unstapled and Must-Staple clients reject. Force a staple refresh on lb-2.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "nginx", "host": "lb-2", "action": "refresh_ocsp_staple"}
System Response: lb-2 fetched a fresh OCSP response (status=good, nextUpdate +4d) and now staples every handshake; Firefox OCSP errors cleared across the pool.
Resolution: Root cause was one LB node with an expired OCSP staple cache and a failing responder fetch; refreshing its staple restored Must-Staple handshakes without disabling stapling.

Reasoning: Ready=False with a stuck Order usually means the ACME challenge will not validate. Start at the Certificate to see the blocking condition and linked Order.
<|call:get_certificate_resource|> {"namespace": "monitoring", "name": "grafana-tls"}
System Response: Ready=False reason=DoesNotExist; current secret valid 32h; latest CertificateRequest -> Order 'grafana-tls-2-3897' state=pending.
Recovery reasoning: The Order is pending — inspect its challenge to find why ACME is blocked.
<|call:describe_acme_order|> {"namespace": "monitoring", "order": "grafana-tls-2-3897"}
System Response: HTTP-01 challenge 'grafana.shopflow.io' state=pending; presented token path returns 404; solver Ingress created but route not matched by ingress-nginx.
Recovery reasoning: The solver Ingress is not being routed (ingressClass mismatch), so the CA cannot reach the token. Patch the Issuer solver to the correct ingressClassName and let the Order retry.
<|call:patch_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "Issuer", "name": "letsencrypt-prod", "namespace": "monitoring", "patch": {"solvers.http01.ingress.class": "nginx"}, "rollout": false}
System Response: solver updated; challenge re-presented, token 200; Order state=valid; Certificate grafana-tls Ready=True, secret rotated, notAfter +90d.
Resolution: Root cause was the ACME HTTP-01 solver using a wrong ingressClass so the challenge 404'd; fixing the solver class let the Order validate and renew before the old cert lapsed.

Reasoning: A tenant domain getting the default '*.shopflow.io' cert on a shared IP means SNI is not selecting the tenant vhost — likely a missing server block falling through to default. Fetch the cert actually served for that SNI.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "app.acme-tenant.com", "sni": "app.acme-tenant.com"}
System Response: served CN=*.shopflow.io (default server cert); expected tenant cert for acme-tenant.com — default vhost is answering.
Recovery reasoning: Either the tenant vhost is absent or its server_name does not match. Query the proxy config for a server block matching this host.
<|call:query_proxy_config|> {"match": "server_name app.acme-tenant.com"}
System Response: no server block matches 'app.acme-tenant.com'; tenant cert secret 'acme-tenant-tls' exists and is valid but is referenced by no vhost.
Recovery reasoning: The cert was provisioned but the vhost wiring was never added, so SNI fell through to default. Add the vhost binding the tenant cert and reload.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "nginx", "add_vhost": {"server_name": "app.acme-tenant.com", "cert": "acme-tenant-tls"}, "validate_first": true}
System Response: config test OK; reloaded; SNI app.acme-tenant.com now serves CN=acme-tenant.com; warning gone; other tenants unaffected.
Resolution: Root cause was a provisioned tenant cert with no matching server block, so SNI fell through to the default '*.shopflow.io' vhost; adding the vhost binding fixed routing without touching other tenants.

Reasoning: 'key values mismatch' means the deployed key does not pair with the deployed cert — a modulus mismatch. Compare cert and key modulus on the node.
<|call:compare_modulus|> {"host": "gw-edge-3", "cert": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.crt", "key": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.key"}
System Response: cert modulus md5=8a3f...; key modulus md5=1c90...; MISMATCH — this key does not sign this cert.
Recovery reasoning: Find which artifact is wrong — check whether the on-node key matches the PREVIOUS cert, which would mean the key is stale.
<|call:compare_modulus|> {"host": "gw-edge-3", "cert": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.crt.prev", "key": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.key"}
System Response: prev-cert modulus md5=1c90... == key modulus md5=1c90... MATCH — the on-node key is the OLD key; the new cert came from a fresh CSR/key that was never shipped.
Recovery reasoning: The release shipped the new cert but left the matching new key behind. Pull the correct pair from the secret store and install atomically to avoid a partial-pair window.
<|call:rotate_tls_secret|> {"host": "gw-edge-3", "source": "vault:secret/gw-edge/tls", "atomic": true, "reload": true}
System Response: matching cert+key installed (cert modulus md5 == key modulus md5 = 5d2b...); nginx reload OK; gw-edge-3 health green, handshakes succeed.
Resolution: Root cause was a half-shipped rotation — new cert with the old private key (modulus mismatch); installing the matching key from the secret store fixed it, the atomic write preventing a partial-pair window.

Reasoning: A valid leaf yet an 'expired' error on the old service, with newer services fine, is the signature of an expired ROOT in the old service's bundled CA store. Inspect which chain element is expired from notify-svc's view.
<|call:inspect_chain|> {"host": "api.vendor-sms.com", "from": "notify-svc", "port": 443}
System Response: leaf OK (Oct), intermediate OK; ROOT 'Legacy Root CA X1' expired 2026-06-27T14:00Z — notify-svc trusts the expired cross-signed root, not the newer root.
Recovery reasoning: notify-svc pins an old ca-bundle that chains to the just-expired legacy root. The vendor already cross-signs to a newer root present on modern hosts. Check notify-svc's trust store for that newer root.
<|call:inspect_trust_store|> {"host": "notify-svc", "ca": "Vendor Root CA G2"}
System Response: 'Vendor Root CA G2' NOT present; bundle is ca-certificates dated 2023, containing only the now-expired Legacy Root CA X1.
Recovery reasoning: The service's CA bundle is stale and lacks the modern root. Update it to current ca-certificates so the chain re-anchors to the unexpired root; do not disable verification.
<|call:update_trust_bundle|> {"host": "notify-svc", "source": "ca-certificates-2026.06", "restart": true}
System Response: bundle updated; 'Vendor Root CA G2' now trusted; chain rebuilds leaf->intermediate->G2 (valid 2031); notify-svc calls return 200.
Resolution: Root cause was a stale CA bundle still anchored to a root that expired at 14:00 UTC; updating ca-certificates re-anchored the chain to the current root without weakening verification.

Reasoning: Web fine, only old app builds failing with 'pinning validation failed' right after rotation means the new leaf's public-key pin is not in the shipped apps' pin set. Confirm the served leaf's SPKI pin.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "api.shopflow.io", "field": "spki_sha256"}
System Response: served leaf SPKI pin sha256=K7m... (new keypair from the rotation); cert valid, chain trusted.
Recovery reasoning: The rotation rekeyed (new SPKI) and old apps pin the previous key, so they hard-fail. We cannot push new pins to shipped apps quickly. Check what those old builds actually pin.
<|call:list_pinned_keys|> {"app_min_version": "<4.2", "host": "api.shopflow.io"}
System Response: old apps pin set = [sha256=J2a... (previous leaf key), sha256=B0c... (backup pin = intermediate SPKI)]; served pin K7m... matches neither.
Recovery reasoning: Reissue the leaf reusing the PREVIOUS keypair under the same intermediate so the served SPKI returns to J2a..., matching shipped pins, while a proper pin-rotation app update is scheduled.
<|call:reissue_certificate|> {"cn": "api.shopflow.io", "reuse_key": "prev-leaf-key", "keep_intermediate": true, "reload": true}
System Response: leaf reissued with the prior keypair (SPKI=J2a...) under the same intermediate; served pin now matches the old-app set; mobile errors drop to ~0; new apps still OK.
Resolution: Root cause was a rekey during rotation invalidating the SPKI pin baked into shipped apps; reissuing with the previous key and unchanged intermediate restored the pin pending a real pin-rotation release.

Reasoning: 'dh key too small' under OpenSSL 3 means the server offers a sub-2048-bit DH group the new client's security level rejects. Probe the DHE parameters the legacy server sends.
<|call:enumerate_ciphers|> {"host": "mainframe-gw.corp", "port": 443, "show_kex": true}
System Response: negotiated DHE-RSA-AES256-SHA, DH param size=1024 bits; no ECDHE suites offered; client SECLEVEL=2 rejects 1024-bit DH.
Recovery reasoning: The legacy server cannot change for weeks. Before lowering anything, check whether it offers any acceptable stronger group or non-DHE suite.
<|call:enumerate_ciphers|> {"host": "mainframe-gw.corp", "port": 443, "filter": "kex!=DHE-1024"}
System Response: only other suite = AES256-SHA (static RSA kex, no PFS); no ECDHE, no DHE>=2048 available server-side.
Recovery reasoning: No PFS option exists on their side. Apply a per-destination SECLEVEL=1 only for mainframe-gw.corp so 1024-bit DHE is accepted there, leaving global SECLEVEL=2 intact, and flag the PFS gap.
<|call:update_cipher_policy|> {"scope": "egress", "host": "mainframe-gw.corp", "openssl_seclevel": 1, "min_version": "TLS1.2"}
System Response: per-host egress policy applied; report-api negotiates DHE-RSA-AES256-SHA to mainframe-gw.corp; all other egress stays SECLEVEL=2; integration restored.
Resolution: Root cause was a 1024-bit DH group on the unpatched legacy server hitting OpenSSL 3.2's SECLEVEL=2 floor; a host-scoped SECLEVEL=1 exception restored the link without lowering global TLS security.

Reasoning: The replacement is deployed yet users still see 'revoked' — likely some edge still serves the revoked cert. Check the serial served and its revocation status.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "partner-portal.shopflow.io", "field": "serial"}
System Response: served serial=0x9F...new; OCSP status=good; cert valid 89d — the node we hit is serving the NEW cert.
Recovery reasoning: This edge is clean, so the errors come from other nodes or caches still holding the revoked cert. Check serials across the whole CDN/edge pool, not one node.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "partner-portal.shopflow.io", "field": "serial", "across_pool": true}
System Response: 5/7 edges serve the new serial (good); 2 edges (cdn-pop-fra, cdn-pop-sin) still serve old serial=0x4A...old, OCSP status=REVOKED.
Recovery reasoning: Two CDN POPs cached the old revoked cert and missed the deploy. Purge their TLS cert cache and force a re-pull from origin so they serve the new serial; the revocation stays enforced because the old key is compromised.
<|call:rotate_tls_secret|> {"host": "cdn-pop-fra,cdn-pop-sin", "action": "purge_and_pull", "source": "origin", "reload": true}
System Response: both POPs pulled new serial 0x9F...; OCSP good on all 7 edges; 'sec_error_revoked_certificate' cleared globally.
Resolution: Root cause was two CDN POPs still serving the revoked (compromised-key) cert after rotation; purging and re-pulling synced them to the new cert while the revocation stayed enforced for safety.
